Subject: [PATCH] fix(rocisa): split 64-bit add immediates correctly (#13027)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

JIRA ID : AIHPBLAS-5022

Independent of the fast_check stack; based on `develop`. The
address-carry lint that started out in this PR now has its own, #13060.

## Summary

While looking at how rocisa lowers 64-bit adds on targets without a
native one (gfx90a, gfx942, gfx950), we noticed that `splitSrcs` copies
an immediate into both 32-bit halves. That's right for the packed-F32
operations that share it, but for a 64-bit `ptr += imm` it would also
add `imm × 2^32` and move the pointer by hundreds of GiB. The vector
form also put the immediate where gfx9 can't encode a literal, so most
immediates wouldn't even assemble.

This PR fixes that lowering with a new `splitSrcs64`, puts a vector
immediate where the hardware can encode it, and adds tests that check,
and assemble, the emitted carry chain on gfx90a, gfx942 and gfx950.
Nothing on develop lowers an immediate through this path yet (the
immediate callers are gfx1250 TDM code, and gfx1250 has a native 64-bit
add), so this closes the door before anyone walks through it. The
lowering tests failed 45 of the original 57 cases before the fix, and
every case passes after it.

**CI time added**: about 7 s in the rocisa tests (`tox -e rocisa`), most
of it assembling the emitted pairs.

## Motivation

Classes C and C2 on AIHPBLAS-4988 are mistakes in emitted 64-bit address
arithmetic, so some can be caught before a kernel ever runs. There have
been at least three 64-bit address bugs in a few weeks: ROCM-31230;
ROCM-32046, where the carry out of the low dword is dropped; and the
launch-size narrowing in ROCM-31290. A lowering that adds `imm × 2^32`
to a pointer would be another, so it's worth fixing while nothing uses
it.

## Technical Details

- **Splitting the immediate.** On targets without `s_add_u64` or
`v_add_nc_u64`, `SAddU64` and `VAddNCU64` lower to a 32-bit carry chain.
A new `splitSrcs64` splits each source for the two 64-bit adds:
  - a register pair splits into its two registers;
- a 32-bit immediate gets its sign extension (0 or -1) in the high half;
- a larger one arrives from Python as a double, and splits as a 64-bit
integer up to 2^53 in magnitude, where that double is still exact;
anything larger is rejected, since it may already have been rounded;
  - a symbol it cannot split is rejected.

The packed operations keep `splitSrcs`. An immediate above 2^31 used to
reach the assembly as a double (`s_add_u32 s4, s4, 4294967299.0`).
- **Where a vector immediate goes.** VOP2 takes a literal only in src0,
and VOP3 on gfx9 takes none, so `v_add_co_u32 v4, vcc, v4, <literal>`
does not assemble. The lowered `VAddNCU64` now puts the immediate in
src0 (the add is commutative). `v_addc_co_u32` reads vcc as well, which
leaves no room for a literal at all, so an immediate whose high half is
not an inline constant (−16 to 64) is rejected with a message; every int
immediate has a high half of 0 or −1. `SAddU64` (SOP2) takes a literal
in either position, so it is unchanged.
- **Tests.** `rocisa/test/test_lowering64.py` checks the emitted pair on
gfx90a, gfx942 and gfx950. For register operands, the low add must set
the carry and the high add must consume it, from the next registers. For
immediates (positive, negative, at ±2^31, above 2^32 and at ±(2^53 −
1)), the high half must be the sign extension or the true high 32 bits.
Symbolic immediates, immediates of 2^53 or more in magnitude, and vector
immediates with a non-inline high half must be rejected. Every emitted
scalar and vector pair is also assembled with `amdclang++` for each
target, since a string check can't see an operand the hardware cannot
encode.
- **Hand-written kernel limits** are already checked on develop by
`Tensile/Tests/unit/test_CustomKernels_buffer_oob.py`, from the
ROCM-31258 fix, so this PR doesn't duplicate that.
- **Race detection (class G).** `rocjitsu race check (gfx94X-dcgpu)`
already runs on rocm-libraries PRs
(`therock-rocjitsu-race-check-linux.yml`), and RocJITsu is what found
the GitHub PR 12605 race. Two gaps: the job is gfx94X only, while the
ROCM-31905 single-LDS-buffer kernels are on gfx1250; and on recent PRs
it fails during setup (`requirements-test.txt` not found) before
checking anything. I'd fix the setup and add gfx950 and gfx1250 coverage
for those kernel families rather than add a second race detector. I
haven't changed the workflow here.

## Test Plan

- `test_lowering64.py` before and after the fix, and with the vector
immediate left in src1.
- The full rocisa and TensileLite unit and characterization suites.

## Test Result

In a ROCm 10.2.0 image (TheRock 10.2.0a20260928), on a gfx90a host, at
2ac9fca2844:

- `test_lowering64.py`: 117 passed on gfx90a, gfx942 and gfx950,
including the assembled pairs. With the vector immediate left in src1,
33 cases fail (assembler: "literal operands are not supported"). With
the old `splitSrcs`, 45 of the original 57 cases failed (every immediate
case, plus the symbol case).
- Full TensileLite unit and rocisa suites: 10,174 passed, 380 skipped, 2
xfailed, 1 xpassed, none failed. With this host's ROCm 7.1 toolchain the
same suites had 157 failures and 14 errors on develop, almost all
gfx1250, TDM and MX code it cannot assemble.
- Automated GitHub checks are pending.

